# Topic guides added to each Gumroad page (original writing, general guidance, no claims of results).
G = {}
G['glow-up-planner'] = ('How to do a 30-day glow up without dieting', [
('Start with how you want to feel, not how you want to look', 'A glow up that starts from a mirror usually turns into a list of flaws to fix. A gentler starting point is a feeling: rested, put-together, confident walking into a room. Write that feeling down first. Every task you pick for the month should move you a little closer to it. If a task does not, drop it.'),
('Pick two areas, not ten', 'Routines, skin, style, mindset, home, money: trying to upgrade all of them at once is the fastest way to quit by day five. Score each area from 1 to 10 and choose the lowest one or two. Thirty days of focus on two areas does more than thirty days of scattered effort across eight.'),
('Make the daily task tiny', 'The best glow up tasks take under fifteen minutes: drink a glass of water before coffee, lay out tomorrow\'s outfit, write one thing you did well, do your skincare even when tired. Tiny tasks build the habit of showing up for yourself, and that habit is the real glow up.'),
('Build a routine you can repeat on your worst day', 'Write a morning and a night routine of three to six steps. Then ask: could I do this on a bad day? If not, cut it down. A short routine done every day beats an ideal routine done twice. Add steps only after the short version is automatic.'),
('Track kindly', 'Tick days, not failures. If you miss a day, do today\'s task and move on; there is no need to catch up. At the end of each week, note one thing that felt easier than before. Those small notes are what you will read on day 30 to see how far you came.'),
('Review, then decide what stays', 'On day 30, look back at your vision page and Glow Wheel. Which habits stuck without effort? Keep those. Which never happened? Let them go without guilt. Then design next month\'s challenge around what actually worked for you.')])
G['sunday-reset-bundle'] = ('How to do a Sunday reset (step by step)', [
('What a Sunday reset is', 'A Sunday reset is a short weekly ritual to close the week you just had and set up the one coming. It usually covers four things: your head (a brain dump), your home (a quick clean of the rooms that matter), your food (a meal plan and shopping list) and your week (your top priorities). Done regularly, it turns Monday from a scramble into a start.'),
('Step 1: Empty your head (10 minutes)', 'Write down every open loop: tasks, worries, things to buy, messages to answer. You are not solving them yet, just getting them out of your head and onto paper. Then circle the three that matter most this week.'),
('Step 2: Reset your space (45 to 60 minutes)', 'Do not try to clean the whole house. Pick the two or three rooms that most affect your week, usually the kitchen, bathroom and bedroom. Work top to bottom in each room so dust and crumbs fall onto surfaces you have not cleaned yet. Put on music and set a timer.'),
('Step 3: Plan your food (15 minutes)', 'Choose dinners for the week first, then breakfasts and lunches. Check what you already have, and write a grocery list by store section so shopping is quick. Note anything you can prep tonight, like washing vegetables or cooking grains.'),
('Step 4: Reset yourself', 'Pick one thing from a self-care menu that fits the time you have left: a bath, a face mask, a walk, a chapter of a book. A reset that only cleans the house and never refills you tends to feel like a second job.'),
('Step 5: Set up Monday', 'Write your top three priorities for the week and one intention, such as "slow mornings" or "say no to extra plans". Lay out clothes, charge devices and pack your bag. Then stop. A reset should end with free time, not more chores.')])
G['self-care-planner'] = ('How to plan self-care so it actually happens', [
('Schedule it like an appointment', 'Self-care that waits for free time rarely happens, because free time rarely appears. Treat it like a meeting: give it a day and a time. Even 15 minutes counts. On a weekly planner, write the me-time slot first, before work and errands fill the page.'),
('Know your kinds of rest', 'Sleep is only one kind of rest. Many people also need mental rest (a break from decisions), sensory rest (less noise and screens), social rest (time alone or with easy people) and creative rest (something that inspires you). Notice which one you are short on this week and plan for that one.'),
('Use a menu when you are tired', 'When you are drained, deciding what to do for yourself is one more decision. A self-care menu sorted by time (5 minutes, 15 minutes, 1 hour, half a day) removes the decision: look at how much time you have and pick one item.'),
('Track mood, not perfection', 'A simple daily mood colour helps you spot patterns: maybe Thursdays are always low, or you feel better after a walk. Patterns tell you where self-care will make the biggest difference.'),
('Check in once a week', 'At the end of the week, ask three questions: what helped, what drained me, and what one thing will I change next week? Keep the answers short. Small adjustments every week add up to a calmer month.'),
('When self-care is not enough', 'Planners help with routines and rest. They are not a substitute for professional help. If low mood, anxiety or exhaustion stick around, talk to a doctor or a mental-health professional.')])
G['monthly-habit-tracker'] = ('How to use a habit tracker (and actually stick with it)', [
('Choose fewer, smaller habits', 'The most common reason habit trackers fail is too many habits at once. Start with three to five. Make each one small and specific: "read 10 pages" instead of "read more", "phone off by 10pm" instead of "sleep better".'),
('Anchor each habit to something you already do', 'Habits stick better when they follow an existing routine: stretch after brushing your teeth, write your gratitude line after you pour your coffee. Write the anchor next to the habit on your tracker.'),
('Mark it immediately', 'Fill the circle the moment you finish the habit, not at night from memory. Keep the tracker somewhere visible, like the fridge, your desk or the inside of a planner you open daily.'),
('Missed a day? Never miss twice', 'One missed day does not undo a month. The useful rule is simply to not miss two days in a row. On paper, a gap is one empty circle, not a broken streak that resets to zero.'),
('Review monthly and adjust', 'At the end of each month, total each row. Keep habits that are near-automatic, shrink the ones you kept missing, and add at most one new habit. Over a year, this slow approach builds more lasting change than starting over every January.'),
('Look at the whole year', 'A year-at-a-glance view shows trends no single month can: the months you were strongest, the seasons that are harder, and how your best streak grew. It is also a good reminder of progress on days that feel stuck.')])
G['new-year-reset-2027'] = ('How to do a new year reset (instead of resolutions)', [
('Reflect before you plan', 'Before setting 2027 goals, look honestly at 2026. List your wins, rate your year across a few life areas (health, relationships, work, money, home, fun), and write down the lessons. This turns goals into a response to your real year, not a copy of someone else\'s list.'),
('Let go on purpose', 'Write a short letter to the old year: what you are proud of, what you are leaving behind, and what you are taking with you. It sounds small, but naming what to release makes room for what comes next.'),
('Do the practical reset', 'A reset is also physical and digital: clear your camera roll, declutter one space, unsubscribe from emails you never read, update passwords, review subscriptions, refresh your morning and night routines. Spread these over a week so it stays enjoyable.'),
('Choose a word, then three goals', 'A word of the year (calm, brave, steady) guides small daily choices. Then choose just three goals. For each, write four concrete first steps. If a goal has no first step you could do this week, it is still a wish.'),
('Give every month a focus', 'Most goals fade by February. A single focus for each month, such as "January: routines, February: money check, March: declutter", keeps momentum without overwhelming you.'),
('Track a few habits for 30 days', 'Pick up to five habits that support your goals and track them for 30 days. After a month you will know which ones fit your life and which to rethink.')])
G['self-care-journal-prompts'] = ('How to start journaling for self-care', [
('You do not need to write well', 'Journaling for self-care is not writing for an audience. Spelling, grammar and neat handwriting do not matter. The point is to notice what you think and feel, and to give it somewhere to go.'),
('Use one prompt, not a blank page', 'A blank page invites overthinking. One clear question, like "What made me smile today?" or "What do I need more of?", gives your mind a place to start. Answer it honestly, even if the answer is short.'),
('Set a small, regular time', 'Ten minutes is enough. Pick a time you already have, like morning coffee, a lunch break or Sunday evening. Consistency matters more than length.'),
('Start light, go deeper later', 'Begin with gentle prompts about small joys and gratitude. As journaling becomes a habit, move to bigger questions about boundaries, values and what you want next. That gradual shift makes the deeper prompts feel less heavy.'),
('Close with one word', 'Ending each entry with one word for the day helps you see patterns over weeks: calm, tired, hopeful, scattered. Looking back at those words is often surprisingly useful.'),
('Know its limits', 'Journaling can help you reflect and process everyday feelings. It is not a replacement for professional support. If something you write about feels too heavy to carry alone, please reach out to someone you trust or a professional.')])
G['morning-night-routine-checklist'] = ('How to build a morning and night routine that fits your life', [
('Start from your real schedule', 'Work backwards from the time you must leave the house or start work, and forwards from the time you want to be asleep. That gives you the real window for a morning routine and a wind-down, rather than a fantasy 5am start.'),
('Time every step', 'Write how many minutes each step takes and add them up. Many routines fail because they need 70 minutes when you only have 30. Seeing the total helps you cut steps before they cost you.'),
('Wind down in stages', 'A good night often starts two hours before bed: finish heavy tasks and eat. One hour before: dim lights, tidy, prepare tomorrow. At lights out: screens away and something calm. Stages make it easier than trying to switch off all at once.'),
('Have a busy-day version', 'Decide your two or three non-negotiables, for example water, skincare and making the bed. On hectic days, do only those. Keeping a minimum routine protects the habit.'),
('Track wake-up and lights-out', 'Logging the times you actually wake and sleep for a week shows whether your routine is realistic. Small shifts of 15 minutes are easier to keep than big jumps.'),
('Tweak one thing a week', 'At the end of the week, change just one thing: move a step, cut a step or add one. Routines that evolve slowly tend to last.')])
G['winter-glow-up-checklist'] = ('How to feel your best in winter: a gentle glow up plan', [
('Chase daylight', 'Short days affect energy for many people. Getting outside in the morning, opening curtains straight away and taking a lunchtime walk are simple ways to get more daylight. Put "morning light" on your checklist so it happens on purpose.'),
('Care for skin and lips in dry air', 'Cold air outside and heating inside both dry out skin. Many people switch to a richer moisturizer, keep lip balm close and use a humidifier. Treat it as part of a cozy routine, not a chore. (For ongoing skin concerns, see a dermatologist.)'),
('Keep moving, gently', 'Winter is a good time for movement that feels nice: walks, stretching, dancing in the kitchen, a home yoga video. Aim for consistent and pleasant rather than intense.'),
('Eat warm, regular meals', 'Soups, stews and warm breakfasts make winter feel comforting. Planning a few go-to meals for busy weeks helps you eat well when energy is low. No calorie counting needed.'),
('Plan rest before the holidays plan you', 'December fills up fast. Block rest days on your calendar first, then add events around them. Decide in advance what you will say yes to and what gets a kind "no, thank you".'),
('Stay connected', 'Shorter days can make it easy to withdraw. Schedule small connections: a call with a friend, a cozy movie night, a walk with someone. Connection is as much a part of a winter glow up as skincare.')])
G['glow-theory-bundle'] = ('How to build a simple self-care system with printables', [
('Think weekly, then daily', 'A self-care system works best with a weekly anchor and small daily actions. The weekly anchor is a Sunday reset: clean a little, plan meals, set priorities and book time for yourself. The daily actions are a short morning and night routine and one tick on a habit tracker.'),
('Plan me-time first', 'When you plan the week, write your self-care time before tasks fill the page. Even 15 minutes a day adds up to nearly two hours a week that belong to you.'),
('Track lightly', 'Track three to five habits, not twenty. A monthly view shows progress without the pressure of daily streak apps.'),
('Reflect once a week', 'One journal prompt a week is enough to notice what is helping and what is draining you. Over a few months, those notes become a record of how you have grown.'),
('Use seasonal resets', 'Winter and the new year are natural moments to reset. Use them to review routines, let go of what is not working and choose a few gentle goals.'),
('Keep it printable and flexible', 'Print only the pages you need. If a page does not help, skip it. The system should fit your life, not the other way round.')])
G['free-self-care-checklist'] = ('A simple self-care checklist: daily, weekly and monthly ideas', [
('Daily basics', 'Daily self-care is mostly maintenance: drink water, move your body, eat a real meal, get some daylight, take a few minutes for something you enjoy, and go to bed at a reasonable time. Ticking these off keeps the foundation steady.'),
('Weekly rituals', 'Once a week: a reset of your space, a meal plan, a self-care night (bath, face mask, a film you love), a check-in with a friend and a review of the week ahead. Weekly rituals stop small things from piling up.'),
('Monthly check-ins', 'Once a month: review your goals, declutter one area, check your budget and subscriptions, and book any appointment you have been putting off. Monthly check-ins catch what daily and weekly routines miss.'),
('Start with two', 'Do not start everything at once. Choose two daily items and one weekly ritual for the first month. Add more only when those feel automatic.'),
('Make it visible', 'Print the checklist and put it where you will see it: the fridge, your desk or the bathroom mirror. Visibility is half the habit.'),
('When to level up', 'If the checklist helps, a weekly planner or a Sunday reset routine can build on it. If it does not, simplify further. Self-care should make life lighter, not heavier.')])
